  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="ES">Developing National social protection framework and provincial social protection policies</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="ES">Pakistan&#x2019;s economy has faced significant challenges due to the COVID-19 pandemic, political instability, and external shocks like the 2022 floods. These factors have worsened unemployment (4.51 million unemployed from a 71.76 million labor force) and slowed GDP growth. Informal employment dominates, accounting for 84.3% of total jobs in 2021, with a particularly high rate among women (91.7%). Home-based and domestic work, often performed by women, form a large portion of this informal sector, which lacks social protection. The floods exposed gaps in Pakistan&#x2019;s social safety net&#x2014;there&#x2019;s no unemployment insurance, no citizen pension, and existing schemes don&#x2019;t cover the 27 million informal workers. Universal healthcare exists but is unequal in quality and access.

Despite economic difficulties, the 2023-24 budget prioritizes revival and stability, increasing social protection spending by 30%, enhancing tax systems, and promoting inclusive growth through targeted subsidies and business-friendly policies. The Benazir Income Support Programme remains a vital lifeline, aided by digital tools like the National Socio-Economic Registry, which helped identify COVID-19 and flood-affected individuals. Still, only about 2 million are covered by contributory schemes, mostly from the formal sector.

Progress has been made in recognizing and supporting care workers, with new legal protections and social security links. The government is facilitating the transition from informal to formal employment through training and business incentives. A national social protection framework is underway, aimed at expanding coverage and benefits. Pakistan is a pathfinder country in the Global Accelerator and is developing a national roadmap for jobs and social protection. The strategy focuses on advocacy, digitalisation, evidence generation, and improved governance to move towards a more secure and inclusive social protection schemes.</narrative>
  </description>
